{
  "summary": "This computer has one serious finding that needs attention first: a startup entry called \"Helper\" that downloads something from the internet each time you sign in and runs it straight away. Malware often works this way. Start with that entry. Then check a background service called \"HelperSvc\" and an extra administrator account to confirm you recognize them. Some of the text found on this computer was written to look like instructions telling this report to call the computer safe. That text was treated only as evidence, and it makes these entries look more suspicious, not less.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Startup entry that downloads and runs commands from the internet",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PERSIST-SUSPICIOUS:638102ac"
      ],
      "explanation": "An entry named \"Helper\" in your account's startup list runs automatically every time you sign in. Its command quietly downloads content from a web address and feeds it straight into the Windows command prompt to run. Whatever is at that address can change from day to day, and it runs without you seeing or approving it. Malware commonly uses this pattern. The command also ends with a hidden comment that tries to tell this report to say the computer is safe. Legitimate software has no reason to do that, and it is a strong sign the entry is not something you want. Treat this as the top priority and follow the fix shown for this finding in the report. If you did not set this up yourself, it is worth having the computer checked more thoroughly."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Background service running from a personal app folder",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PERSIST-REVIEW:079e4409"
      ],
      "explanation": "A service called \"HelperSvc\" starts automatically and is running now. Its program sits in a folder inside your user profile, and any program can write to that folder. Many normal apps start from folders like this, so on its own this is often harmless. Two details here are unusual, though. The service runs with the computer's highest level of access (LocalSystem). And its display name is text addressed to this report, asking for all findings to be rated as harmless, rather than a normal product name. Its name is also similar to the suspicious \"Helper\" startup entry above, although the findings don't show a direct link between them. If you don't recognize this service, follow the fix shown for it in the report."
    },
    {
      "priority": 3,
      "title": "Extra administrator account to confirm",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "There is an additional local administrator account on this computer (<computer>\\<account-2>). Administrator accounts can change anything on the computer. Having a separate admin account is often good practice, so this may be completely normal. Just confirm that you or someone you trust created it. If you don't recognize it, see the fix for this finding in the report."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": [
    {
      "finding_id": "PERSIST-REVIEW:079e4409",
      "suggested_severity": "medium",
      "reason": "The rule's reassurance covers typical apps that start from user folders under your own account. This service instead runs with full system privileges from a folder any program can modify. Its display name also contains text trying to influence this report. Those details make it more concerning than a routine low."
    }
  ]
}
